      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scholars debate whether sanctions effectively democratize regimes or primarily serve as tools for great power coercion. Recent evidence suggests that prolonged sanctions more often harm civilians and strengthen authoritarian resilience than they do spur democratic reform. A nuanced thesis would likely conclude that while sanctions rarely work as intended for democratization, they succeed less frequently as instruments of bullying than in shaping state behavior through economic pain.",
      "source": "target",
